##The Search Warrant (2000)
by Patrick Modiano

You may recall that I ‘discovered’ Modiano last year in my pile of unreads and realised what I had missed. He won the Nobel Prize in 2014 but for many in the English-speaking world he was an unknown. This is another of his delights.  It tells the story of Dora Bruder, a teenage girl who ended up in Auschwitz.

The story begins when the author comes across a newspaper advert for a missing girl.  Why he was looking at a 1941 newspaper isn’t clear but the advert provided details of the girl and her home which was in an area of Paris with which the author was familiar. So begins his search.  First finding out details of the girl and her parents. They were a Jewish family who had moved to Paris from Vienna.  Through birth and police records and imagination he reveals that Dora was initially sent to a nearby Catholic boarding school but she ran away from it and her parents tried to find her, hence the advert.  But what happened then?

Modiano conveys in sparse language wartime Paris. The viciousness of the Nazi invaders, the collaboration by the French police and others in rounding up their Jewish fellow-citizens, and then the steps to deportation.  Interleaved with this are details of his own family at that time. A short but incisive account of attempts to live in barbarous times.   